1
00:00:34,088 --> 00:00:39,249
<v ED> Did you make notes while you were watching the performances of Romeo and Juliet, Gemma?

2
00:00:39,259 --> 00:00:42,816
<v GEMMA> Yes, I did. I found it quite hard though.

3
00:00:42,826 --> 00:00:45,150
<v GEMMA> I kept getting too involved in the play.

4
00:00:45,160 --> 00:00:48,313
<v ED> Me too. I ended up not taking notes.

5
00:00:48,323 --> 00:00:50,865
<v ED> I wrote down my impressions when I got home.

6
00:00:50,875 --> 00:00:53,867
<v ED> Do you mind if I check a few things with you?

7
00:00:53,877 --> 00:00:55,387
<v ED> In case I've missed anything.

8
00:00:55,397 --> 00:00:59,063
<v ED> And I've also got some questions about our assignment.

9
00:00:59,073 --> 00:01:02,004
<v GEMMA> No, it's good to talk things through.

10
00:01:02,014 --> 00:01:03,854
<v GEMMA> I may have missed things too.

11
00:01:03,864 --> 00:01:10,909
<v ED> OK great. So first of all, I'm not sure how much information we should include in our reviews.

12
00:01:10,919 --> 00:01:15,642
<v GEMMA> Right. Well, I don't think we need to describe what happens.

13
00:01:15,652 --> 00:01:20,559
<v GEMMA> Especially as Romeo and Juliet is one of Shakespeare's most well-known plays.

14
00:01:20,569 --> 00:01:22,641
<v ED> Yeah, everyone knows the story.

15
00:01:22,651 --> 00:01:30,999
<v ED> In an essay we'd focus on the poetry and Shakespeare's use of imagery etc., but that isn't really relevant in a review.

16
00:01:31,009 --> 00:01:35,797
<v ED> We're supposed to focus on how effective this particular production is.

17
00:01:35,807 --> 00:01:40,457
<v GEMMA> Mmm. We should say what made it a success or a failure.

18
00:01:40,467 --> 00:01:45,427
<v ED> And part of that means talking about the emotional impact the performance had on us.

19
00:01:45,437 --> 00:01:46,737
<v ED> I think that's important.

20
00:01:46,747 --> 00:01:57,014
<v GEMMA> Yes. And we should definitely mention how well the director handled important bits of the play-like when Romeo climbs onto Juliet's balcony.

21
00:01:57,024 --> 00:01:59,328
<v ED> And the fight between Mercutio and Tybalt.

22
00:01:59,338 --> 00:02:09,481
<v GEMMA> Yes. It would also be interesting to mention the theatre space and how the director used it but I don't think we'll have space in 800 words.

23
00:02:09,491 --> 00:02:13,383
<v ED> No. OK. That all sounds quite straightforward.

24
00:03:20,259 --> 00:03:24,433
<v ED> So what about The Emporium Theatre's production of the play?

25
00:03:24,443 --> 00:03:29,493
<v GEMMA> I thought some things worked really well but there were some problems too.

26
00:03:29,503 --> 00:03:32,794
<v ED> Yeah. What about the set, for example?

27
00:03:32,804 --> 00:03:35,956
<v GEMMA> I think it was visually really stunning.

28
00:03:35,966 --> 00:03:40,058
<v GEMMA> I'd say that was probably the most memorable thing about this production.

29
00:03:40,068 --> 00:03:40,808
<v ED> You're right.

30
00:03:40,818 --> 00:03:47,651
<v ED>  The set design was really amazing, but actually I have seen similar ideas used in other productions.

31
00:03:47,661 --> 00:03:50,488
<v GEMMA> What about the lighting?

32
00:03:50,498 --> 00:03:54,709
<v GEMMA> Some of the scenes were so dimly lit it was quite hard to see.

33
00:03:54,719 --> 00:03:56,397
<v ED> I didn't dislike it.

34
00:03:56,407 --> 00:03:59,759
<v ED> It helped to change the mood of the quieter scenes.

35
00:03:59,769 --> 00:04:01,168
<v GEMMA> That's a good point.

36
00:04:01,178 --> 00:04:03,517
<v ED> What did you think of the costumes?

37
00:04:03,527 --> 00:04:07,347
<v GEMMA> I was a bit surprised by the contemporary dress, I must say.

38
00:04:07,357 --> 00:04:11,750
<v ED> Yeah - I think it worked well, but I had assumed it would be more conventional.

39
00:04:11,760 --> 00:04:23,811
<v GEMMA> Me too. I liked the music at the beginning and I thought the musicians were brilliant, but I thought they were wasted because the music didn't have much impact in Acts 2 and 3.

40
00:04:23,821 --> 00:04:25,091
<v ED> Yes - that was a shame.

41
00:04:25,101 --> 00:04:30,401
<v GEMMA> One problem with this production was that the actors didn't deliver the lines that well.

42
00:04:30,411 --> 00:04:32,312
<v GEMMA> They were speaking too fast.

43
00:04:32,322 --> 00:04:39,613
<v ED> It was a problem I agree, but I thought it was because they weren't speaking loudly enough - especially at key points in the play.

44
00:04:39,623 --> 00:04:42,355
<v GEMMA> I actually didn't have a problem with that.

45
00:04:42,365 --> 00:04:47,743
<v ED> It's been an interesting experience watching different versions of Romeo and Juliet, hasn't it?

46
00:04:47,753 --> 00:04:52,261
<v GEMMA> Definitely. It's made me realise how relevant the play still is.

47
00:04:52,271 --> 00:04:57,452
<v ED> Right. I mean a lot's changed since Shakespeare's time, but in many ways nothing's changed.

48
00:04:57,462 --> 00:05:02,138
<v ED> There are always disagreements and tension between teenagers and their parents.

49
00:05:02,148 --> 00:05:10,099
<v GEMMA> Yes, that's something all young people can relate to - more than the violence and the extreme emotions in the play.

50
00:05:10,109 --> 00:05:13,076
<v ED> How did you find watching it in translation?

51
00:05:13,086 --> 00:05:14,644
<v GEMMA> Really interesting.

52
00:05:14,654 --> 00:05:19,853
<v GEMMA> I expected to find it more challenging, but I could follow the story pretty well.

53
00:05:19,863 --> 00:05:25,500
<v ED> I stopped worrying about not being able to understand all the words and focused on the actors' expressions.

54
00:05:25,510 --> 00:05:27,747
<v ED> The ending was pretty powerful.

55
00:05:27,757 --> 00:05:31,894
<v GEMMA> Yes. That somehow intensified the emotion for me.

56
00:05:31,904 --> 00:05:37,384
<v ED> Did you know Shakespeare's been translated into more languages than any other writer?

57
00:05:37,394 --> 00:05:42,041
<v GEMMA> What's the reason for his international appeal, do you think?

58
00:05:42,051 --> 00:05:48,340
<v ED> I was reading that it's because his plays are about basic themes that people everywhere are familiar with.

59
00:05:48,350 --> 00:05:52,562
<v GEMMA> Yeah, and they can also be understood on different levels.

60
00:05:52,572 --> 00:05:54,603
<v GEMMA> The characters have such depth.

61
00:05:54,613 --> 00:05:59,034
<v ED> Right - which allows directors to experiment and find new angles.

62
00:05:59,044 --> 00:06:01,343
<v GEMMA> That's really important because ...